# NOETHERVIM: FILES AND WINDOWS

Three ways to reach a file, and one way to arrange what you reached. This
lesson covers the file browser, the pickers, and the window namespace.

It assumes you know the prefixes from
[lesson two](@tutor:noethervim-02-namespaces).

Table of contents:

- [Oil, the editable directory](*oil*)
- [Pickers, for finding by name](*pickers*)
- [Windows and splits](*windows*)
- [Panels](*panels*)
- [Closing what you opened](*closing*)
- [Recap](*recap*)

## OIL, THE EDITABLE DIRECTORY *oil*

Press `<C-w><C-o>`. A floating window opens on the current directory.

The idea worth absorbing: **this is a normal buffer, and the file system is
what it edits**. You do not use a menu of operations.

    rename a file      change its line
    delete a file      `dd` the line
    create a file      add a line
    create a directory add a line ending in `/`
    move a file        `dd` in one directory, `p` in another

Nothing touches disk until you `:w`, and a confirmation lists every change
first. Until then it is ordinary text editing, with ordinary undo.

Moving around:

    `<CR>`    enter a directory, or open a file
    `-`       go to the parent directory
    `g?`      every Oil key, including the ones NoetherVim adds
    `<C-p>`   preview the entry under the cursor, following as you move
    `gV`      a second pane beside this one, for moving files between
            directories; `<C-h>` and `<C-l>` switch panes

Some NoetherVim additions worth knowing: `gf` finds files under this
directory, `gG` greps inside it, `yp` yanks the full path, `g.` toggles
hidden files, and `gd` toggles permissions and sizes.

IMPORTANT `q` does not close Oil, because it is a buffer you are expected to
type into and `q` would shadow macro recording. Leave with `<C-c>` or `ZA`.
If you want `q` anyway, set `q_close = { oil = true }` in your config; see
[q to close](noethervim-q-close).

Oil replaces Neovim's own directory listing, including `:e .`.

Fill in the key that opens it:

The file browser opens with ___

## PICKERS, FOR FINDING BY NAME *pickers*

When you know part of a name, do not navigate; search. Everything here is
under SearchLeader, because none of it changes anything.

    `<Space>ff`   find a file by name
    `<Space>fp`   find a file in the project
    `<Space>fo`   recently opened files
    `<Space>fb`   switch between open buffers
    `<Space>gg`   grep this directory for text
    `<Space>gp`   grep the project
    `<Space>/`    fuzzy search inside this buffer
    `<Space>fh`   search Neovim's help tags
    `<Space>fk`   search every key currently mapped, including plugins

Two habits make these much better.

**`<F1>` lists the keys a picker binds.** Every picker has extras, and they
are not the same from one to the next. The overlay says how to dismiss it in
its own title. See [the picker key list](noethervim-picker-help).

**`<Space>r` resumes the last picker**, with your query still in it, which
is what you want after opening the wrong result.

Fill in the key that lists a picker's own keys:

Inside any picker, the key list is ___

## WINDOWS AND SPLITS *windows*

Everything that makes, moves, sizes or fills a window is under `<C-w>`.

Splitting and moving:

    `<C-w>v`             split vertically
    `<C-w>s`             split horizontally
    `<C-h>` `<C-j>` `<C-k>` `<C-l>`   move to the window in that direction
    `<C-w><M-h>` `<C-w><M-l>`         move this tab left or right

Resizing is on the arrow keys, which is worth trying rather than reading:
an arrow pushes the near edge of the window that way, so the window grows;
`<S-Arrow>` pulls the far edge in, so it shrinks. With only one window and
nothing to resize against, the arrows fall back to moving the cursor. See
[the resize arrows](noethervim-resize-arrows).

Three keys make a split on a scratch buffer, for when you want somewhere to
put text rather than a second view of this file:

    `|`   vertical split, scratch buffer
    `_`   horizontal split, scratch buffer
    `+`   new tab, scratch buffer

Try it now: press `|`, type a word in the new window, then come back with
`<C-h>`. The scratch buffer is real but unnamed, and `ZR` later deletes
every one of them at once.

Tabs are under the bracket pair rather than `<C-w>`, since moving between
them is directional: `]t` and `[t`.

## PANELS *panels*

A panel is a window, so panels are under `<C-w>` too. Each of these toggles.

    `<C-w>t`        a terminal along the bottom, reusing the same one
    `<C-w><C-e>`    the file explorer sidebar
    `<C-w><C-q>`    the quickfix list
    `<C-w>Q`        the diagnostics panel, across every file
    `<C-w><C-u>`    the undo tree
    `<C-w><C-m>`    Mason, which installs language servers and tools
    `<C-w><C-l>`    what the language server is doing
    `<C-w>ll`       lazy.nvim, which manages plugins

In the terminal, `<Esc><Esc>` returns you to normal mode.

## CLOSING WHAT YOU OPENED *closing*

Most of these panels close with a bare `q`, because they are windows you
read rather than type into. Editable buffers deliberately do not, so `q`
keeps its meaning as macro recording where you might actually use it.

For everything else there is the `Z` grid from lesson two: `ZA` closes this
window, `ZD` deletes this buffer, `ZW` leaves Neovim.

